ass="enc-name">Borneo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Borneo is a large island in the Malay Archipelago of Southeast Asia, today divided between Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bornholm"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bornholm.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bornholm</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bornholm is a Danish island in the Baltic Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/borno-state"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/borno-state.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Borno State</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Borno State is a state in north-eastern Nigeria, bordering Niger, Chad and Cameroon, with its capital at Maiduguri.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/bornu"><span class="enc-name">Bornu</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Bornu was a state and region west of Lake Chad, the seat of the Kanem-Bornu Empire after its ruling dynasty moved there from Kanem in the late fourteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/borobudur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/borobudur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Borobudur</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Borobudur is a monumental Mahayana Buddhist temple in central Java, built in the eighth and ninth centuries under the Sailendra dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/borommakot"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/borommakot.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Borommakot</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Borommakot was king of the Ayutthaya Kingdom of Siam from 1733 to 1758, after a violent struggle for the throne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/borommaracha-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/borommaracha-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Borommaracha I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Borommaracha I, also known as Khun Luang Pha Ngua, was king of Ayutthaya from 1370 to 1388.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/borommarachathirat-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/borommarachathirat-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Borommarachathirat I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Borommarachathirat I, earlier Khun Luang Pa-ngua, ruler of Suphanburi, was king of Ayutthaya from 1370 to 1388.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/borommarachathirat-ii"><span class="enc-name">Borommarachathirat II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Borommaracha II, known before his accession as Chao Sam Phraya, was king of Ayutthaya from 1424 to 1448.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat"><span class="enc-name">Borommatrailokkanat</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Borommatrailokkanat was king of Ayutthaya from 1448 to 1488.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/borovsk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/borovsk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Borovsk</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Borovsk is a small town in Kaluga Oblast, Russia, on the Protva river south-west of Moscow.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/borpatrogohain"><span class="enc-name">Borpatrogohain</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Borpatrogohain was a ministerial office of the Ahom kingdom of Assam, created by King Suhungmung in the early 16th century as a third great counsellor beside the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/borre-mound-cemetery"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/borre-mound-cemetery.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Borre mound cemetery</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Borre mound cemetery, in Horten in Vestfold, Norway, is one of the largest royal burial grounds of Scandinavia, with great burial mounds of the 7th to 10th centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/borrell-ii-of-barcelona"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/borrell-ii-of-barcelona.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Borrell II of Barcelona</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Borrell II was Count of Barcelona, Girona and Osona from 947 and Count of Urgell from 948.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/borsippa"><span class="enc-name">Borsippa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Borsippa was an ancient city of Babylonia, about 20 km south-west of Babylon in modern Iraq.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/borso-deste"><span class="enc-name">Borso d'Este</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Borso d'Este was lord of Ferrara from 1450.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/borte"><span class="enc-name">Börte</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Börte was the first and principal wife of Temüjin, later Genghis Khan, and mother of his four main sons, Jochi, Chagatai, Ögedei and Tolui.</span></a></li></ul>
